                    <section id="introduction">
                        <h2>Introduction</h2>
                        <p>In the world of XML processing, XSLT (eXtensible Stylesheet Language Transformations) stands as one of the most powerful tools for converting XML documents into other formats. Whether you need to generate HTML for web display, create PDF documents, or simply restructure your XML data, XSLT provides a declarative language to describe these transformations.</p>
                        <p>This comprehensive guide will walk you through the fundamentals of XSLT, from basic syntax to advanced techniques, equipping you with the knowledge to harness the full power of XML transformations in your projects.</p>
                    </section>

                    <section id="what-is-xslt">
                        <h2>What is XSLT?</h2>
                        <p>XSLT is a language for transforming XML documents into other XML documents, HTML, plain text, or other formats. It's part of the XSL (eXtensible Stylesheet Language) family of technologies developed by the W3C.</p>
                        
                        <div class="bg-purple-50 border-l-4 border-purple-500 p-4 mb-6">
                            <div class="flex">
                                <div class="flex-shrink-0">
                                    <svg class="h-5 w-5 text-purple-600" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20">
                                        <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M18 10a8 8 0 11-16 0 8 8 0 0116 0zm-7-4a1 1 0 11-2 0 1 1 0 012 0zM9 9a1 1 0 000 2v3a1 1 0 001 1h1a1 1 0 100-2v-3a1 1 0 00-1-1H9z" clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                                    </svg>
                                </div>
                                <div class="ml-3">
                                    <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-purple-800">Key Characteristics</h3>
                                    <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-purple-700">
                                        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                                            <li>Declarative language (describes what to do, not how to do it)</li>
                                            <li>Uses pattern matching to select nodes from the source document</li>
                                            <li>Template-based processing model</li>
                                            <li>Functional programming style (no side effects)</li>
                                            <li>Part of the XSL family (along with XPath and XSL-FO)</li>
                                        </ul>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <h3>XSLT in the XML Ecosystem</h3>
                        <p>XSLT works in conjunction with other XML technologies:</p>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li><strong>XML</strong>: The source document to be transformed</li>
                            <li><strong>XPath</strong>: Used to navigate and select nodes from the XML document</li>
                            <li><strong>XSL-FO</strong>: For formatting objects (often used with XSLT to generate PDFs)</li>
                            <li><strong>XSD</strong>: XML Schema can validate both source and transformed documents</li>
                        </ul>
                    </section>

                    <section id="basic-syntax">
                        <h2>Basic Syntax</h2>
                        <p>An XSLT stylesheet is itself an XML document, which means it must be well-formed XML. The root element of an XSLT stylesheet is <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:stylesheet</code> or <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:transform</code> (they are synonyms).</p>
                        
                        <h3>Basic Structure</h3>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;xsl:stylesheet version="1.0" 
    xmlns:xsl="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform"&gt;
    
    &lt;xsl:template match="/"&gt;
        &lt;!-- Transformation rules go here --&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
    
&lt;/xsl:stylesheet&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <h3>Common XSLT Elements</h3>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:template</code>: Defines a processing rule for specific nodes</li>
                            <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:value-of</code>: Extracts the value of a node</li>
                            <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:for-each</code>: Loops through a set of nodes</li>
                            <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:if</code> / <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:choose</code>: Conditional processing</li>
                            <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:apply-templates</code>: Applies templates to child nodes</li>
                            <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:output</code>: Controls the format of the output</li>
                        </ul>
                    </section>

                    <section id="transformation-process">
                        <h2>Transformation Process</h2>
                        <p>XSLT processors work by:</p>
                        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li>Parsing the source XML document into a node tree</li>
                            <li>Reading the XSLT stylesheet and building template rules</li>
                            <li>Starting at the root node and applying matching templates</li>
                            <li>Processing child nodes recursively (unless told otherwise)</li>
                            <li>Building the result tree based on template instructions</li>
                            <li>Serializing the result tree to the output format</li>
                        </ol>

