                    <section id="templates">
                        <h2>Working with Templates</h2>
                        <p>Templates are the heart of XSLT processing. They define how specific nodes in the source document should be transformed.</p>
                        
                        <div class="template-diagram">
                            <h4>Template Processing Flow</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li class="match">
                                    <strong>Pattern Matching</strong>: The XSLT processor finds nodes that match the template's <code>match</code> attribute
                                </li>
                                <li class="apply">
                                    <strong>Template Application</strong>: For each matching node, the processor applies the template's instructions
                                </li>
                                <li class="output">
                                    <strong>Result Generation</strong>: The template generates output nodes that are added to the result tree
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <h3>Template Priorities</h3>
                        <p>When multiple templates match a node, the processor determines which one to use based on:</p>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li><strong>Import precedence</strong>: Templates in imported stylesheets have lower priority</li>
                            <li><strong>Priority attribute</strong>: Explicit priority set with <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">priority</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Pattern specificity</strong>: More specific patterns have higher priority</li>
                            <li><strong>Document order</strong>: Later templates have higher priority if all else is equal</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h3>Built-in Templates</h3>
                        <p>XSLT includes built-in template rules that apply when no explicit template matches:</p>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;!-- For element nodes --&gt;
&lt;xsl:template match="*|/"&gt;
    &lt;xsl:apply-templates/&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;

&lt;!-- For text and attribute nodes --&gt;
&lt;xsl:template match="text()|@*"&gt;
    &lt;xsl:value-of select="."/&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <section id="common-tasks">
                        <h2>Common Transformation Tasks</h2>
                        <p>Here are some typical scenarios you'll encounter when working with XSLT:</p>
                        
                        <h3>1. Converting XML to HTML</h3>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;xsl:template match="/"&gt;
    &lt;html&gt;
        &lt;head&gt;
            &lt;title&gt;Book List&lt;/title&gt;
        &lt;/head&gt;
        &lt;body&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;Book Catalog&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;table border="1"&gt;
                &lt;tr&gt;
                    &lt;th&gt;Title&lt;/th&gt;
                    &lt;th&gt;Author&lt;/th&gt;
                    &lt;th&gt;Price&lt;/th&gt;
                &lt;/tr&gt;
                &lt;xsl:apply-templates select="catalog/book"/&gt;
            &lt;/table&gt;
        &lt;/body&gt;
    &lt;/html&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;

&lt;xsl:template match="book"&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;xsl:value-of select="title"/&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;xsl:value-of select="author"/&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;xsl:value-of select="price"/&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <h3>2. Sorting Data</h3>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;xsl:template match="catalog"&gt;
    &lt;books&gt;
        &lt;xsl:for-each select="book"&gt;
            &lt;xsl:sort select="price" data-type="number" order="descending"/&gt;
            &lt;book&gt;
                &lt;xsl:copy-of select="*"/&gt;
            &lt;/book&gt;
        &lt;/xsl:for-each&gt;
    &lt;/books&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <h3>3. Conditional Processing</h3>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;xsl:template match="book"&gt;
    &lt;div class="book"&gt;
        &lt;xsl:if test="price &gt; 30"&gt;
            &lt;span class="premium"&gt;Premium Book&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;/xsl:if&gt;
        &lt;xsl:choose&gt;
            &lt;xsl:when test="price &lt; 20"&gt;
                &lt;span class="discount"&gt;Budget Price&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;/xsl:when&gt;
            &lt;xsl:otherwise&gt;
                &lt;span class="normal"&gt;Standard Price&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;/xsl:otherwise&gt;
        &lt;/xsl:choose&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <section id="advanced-features">
                        <h2>Advanced Features</h2>
                        <p>XSLT offers several powerful features for complex transformations:</p>
                        
                        <h3>1. Variables and Parameters</h3>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;xsl:variable name="discount" select="0.2"/&gt;
&lt;xsl:param name="currency" select="'USD'"/&gt;

&lt;xsl:template match="price"&gt;
    &lt;price currency="{$currency}"&gt;
        &lt;xsl:value-of select=". * (1 - $discount)"/&gt;
    &lt;/price&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <h3>2. Named Templates</h3>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;xsl:template name="format-price"&gt;
    &lt;xsl:param name="amount"/&gt;
    &lt;xsl:value-of select="format-number($amount, '$#,##0.00')"/&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;

&lt;xsl:template match="price"&gt;
    &lt;xsl:call-template name="format-price"&gt;
        &lt;xsl:with-param name="amount" select="."/&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:call-template&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <h3>3. Multiple Output Documents</h3>
                        <div class="code-block mb-6">
                            <pre><code>&lt;xsl:template match="/"&gt;
    &lt;xsl:for-each select="catalog/book"&gt;
        &lt;xsl:result-document href="book_{position()}.html"&gt;
            &lt;html&gt;
                &lt;body&gt;
                    &lt;h1&gt;&lt;xsl:value-of select="title"/&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
                    &lt;p&gt;By &lt;xsl:value-of select="author"/&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
                &lt;/body&gt;
            &lt;/html&gt;
        &lt;/xsl:result-document&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:for-each&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <section id="best-practices">
                        <h2>Best Practices</h2>
                        <p>Follow these guidelines to create maintainable and efficient XSLT stylesheets:</p>
                        
                        <h3>1. Modular Design</h3>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li>Break complex transformations into separate templates</li>
                            <li>Use <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:include</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:import</code> to reuse common code</li>
                            <li>Create libraries of utility templates for common tasks</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h3>2. Performance Optimization</h3>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li>Use <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:key</code> for efficient node lookups</li>
                            <li>Minimize use of <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">//</code> operator in XPath expressions</li>
                            <li>Cache frequently used values in variables</li>
                            <li>Consider using XSLT 2.0/3.0 for large transformations</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h3>3. Maintainability</h3>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li>Add comments to explain complex logic</li>
                            <li>Use consistent naming conventions</li>
                            <li>Include documentation with <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">xsl:documentation</code></li>
                            <li>Validate your stylesheets with XML Schema</li>
                        </ul>
                    </section>

                    <section id="conclusion">
                        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
                        <p>XSLT is a powerful language for transforming XML documents into various formats. While it has a learning curve, its declarative nature and template-based approach make it ideal for many XML processing tasks.</p>
                        <p>Key takeaways:</p>
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6">
                            <li>XSLT uses pattern matching and templates to transform XML</li>
                            <li>The language is declarative and functional in nature</li>
                            <li>XPath is used extensively to select nodes from the source document</li>
                            <li>XSLT can generate HTML, XML, text, and other formats</li>
                            <li>Modern XSLT processors support versions 1.0, 2.0, and 3.0</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>As you become more comfortable with XSLT, you'll discover its true power in handling complex transformations with relatively simple and maintainable code. Whether you're generating web content, converting between XML vocabularies, or preparing data for other systems, XSLT is a valuable tool in any XML developer's toolkit.</p>
